diagnosis_text:
The reported outage does not reproduce and the fabric is healthy. End-to-end connectivity between the hosts is working: pc_0_0 can ping pc_0_1 and vice versa with 0% loss and ~1.2 ms RTT. The suspected failure (“leaf_router_0_1 lost both uplinks to the spines”) is disproven by direct link and routing evidence on leaf_router_0_1.

Interfaces to both spines are physically and logically up: eth0 (172.16.0.7/31 toward spine_router_0_0) and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31 toward spine_router_0_1) show UP/LOWER_UP, ethtool reports Link detected: yes at 10Gb/s full duplex. BGP is established on both uplinks: “show ip bgp summary” shows two neighbors (172.16.0.6 AS65100 and 172.16.0.10 AS65101) in Established state, each receiving 1 prefix and sending 2. The leaf advertises its local 10.0.1.0/24 and has a BGP-learned route to 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.10 on eth1. This demonstrates intact control-plane and data-plane connectivity across both spine-facing links.

Localization: No faulty device or interface identified. Root cause: No network-side fault present; the “lost uplinks” hypothesis is incorrect. Corrective fix: None required on the fabric. Recommend the user re-test and, if issues persist intermittently, investigate endpoint-specific causes (host firewall, transient app issues) or capture timestamps for correlation; no link or device restart is warranted. 
